{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/974857-where-is-the-horror-of-monsters-in-mage","id":974857,"title":"Where Is The Horror Of Monsters In Mage?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Mage: The Awakening"],"posts":[{"date":"2016-09-22T20:59:29-07:00","id":974857,"title":"Where Is The Horror Of Monsters In Mage?","content":"It goes without saying that mages are going to encounter all kinds of monsters, but that kind of horror doesn't really work in Mage. Horror movie monsters rely on the unknown. Good horror movies keep the monster hidden in the shadows, and never reveal what it actually looks like until the end. Our imaginations are much scarier than anything a costume artist could come up with. But Mages have this covered. Mages can see everything, pinpoint the nature and weaknesses of any given monster, and have all sorts of ways to become nigh-invulnerable, like turning into shadow, or shifting into twilight.<br /> <br /> So this begs the question: Where does the horror in these creatures come from? Is it just about the guilt a mage has from not protecting the monster's victims? There's gotta be more to this right? How do you make monsters scary, or at the very least interesting?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/191-strill","name":"Strill","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1261"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-22T21:11:37-07:00","id":974861,"title":"","content":"I guess one of the big things is that Mages don't all know everything about every monster they encounter. They, like anyone else, have to figure out exactly what it is they're dealing with through encountering/observing it. Sure, they might be able to identify the easily classifiable stuff like vampires and werewolves, but what about all of the other strange stuff in the world that doesn't fit into easy categorization? It's the weird things that lurk in the dark that make up a majority of the supernatural stuff in the setting.<br /> <br /> Yes, a lot of them can be similar to other more familiar things but that only makes it worse when it suddenly busts out a completely new power that is nothing like what you've seen from it's type of creature before. So sure, this might be some sort of undead blood drinking monstrosity that <i>seems</i> kind of like a vampire, but then it possesses none of a vampires weaknesses and you find out at the worst possible time that it appears to have the ability to travel through mirrors and then you've got not idea what you're going to do about that or what the limitations of it's abilities are without further investigation.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2309-ashenrogue","name":"Ashenrogue","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2309_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1388"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-22T21:11:55-07:00","id":974862,"title":"","content":"If they could see everything, they wouldn't be chasing Mysteries.<br /> <br /> And more on point, there is no horror without <i>awareness</i>. You cannot be afraid of a monster that is never known.<br /> <br /> The Ability to See does not absolve mages of horror-it <i>bombards them</i><i> with it</i>.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kelly R.S. Steele, Freelance Writer(Feel free to call me Kelly, Arcane, or Arc)</b><br /> The world is not beautiful, therefore it is.-Keiichi Sigsawa, Kino's Journey<br /> Feminine pronouns, please.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":7}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","name":"ArcaneArts","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar99_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11384"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-22T21:12:45-07:00","id":974864,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Strill</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/974857-where-is-the-horror-of-monsters-in-mage#post974857\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Good horror movies keep the monster hidden in the shadows, and never reveal what it actually looks like until the end.</div> </div> </div> </div>Four words my friend; John Carpenter's The Thing<br /> <br /> Edit: Seven more words; Knowledge and Rationality Will Not Save You","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1705-okami","name":"Okami","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1705_2.gif","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"152"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-22T21:18:08-07:00","id":974870,"title":"","content":"I'm not sure this is as much of a problem in play as it seems to be in theory. I've put my Mage PCs against a couple of weird scary monsters in the course of the game, and they almost never use all the knowing-tools that are supposedly available to them. I also have the luxury of a cabal that doesn't have any dots in say Death or Spirit, so there's a whole class of beings I can put them up against that they can't just scan and understand.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>2E Legacy Updates</b><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/978498-2e-legacy-update-brotherhood-of-the-demon-wind\" target=\"_blank\">Brotherhood of the Demon Wind</a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/979249-2e-legacy-update-choir-of-hashmallim-with-bonus-content-about-summoning\" target=\"_blank\">Choir of Hashmallim</a> (plus extra Summoning content)<br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/980550-2e-legacy-update-storm-keepers\" target=\"_blank\">Storm Keepers</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2207-caladriu","name":"Caladriu","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1048"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-22T21:56:29-07:00","id":974881,"title":"","content":"I mean, you can't ask for better alien horror than Abyssal entities. But Mage gives it to you anyway in the form of Supernal entities.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":8}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-22T22:26:45-07:00","id":974889,"title":"","content":"Extempore are prometheans that are so weird that even in Azothic Memory has no idea what they are.... Mage sight doesn't even know what symbols even make up a normal promethean's power, these guys would be complete black hole when it comes to mages trying to understand them.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/demon-the-descent/904596-geniuses-and-gramarie\" target=\"_blank\">Genius templates</a>: Super Science Mini-Template for Demon: the Descent<br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1337874-oracle-the-endbringers-fan-splat\" target=\"_blank\">Oracle the Endbringers</a>: Time-Manipulator Fan-Splat","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8331-milo-v3","name":"milo v3","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar8331_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1505"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-23T02:20:35-07:00","id":974950,"title":"","content":"Put your Mages in the 2e Hedge, they won't know what hit them. Some of the Arcana seemly won't work properly, like Time and Space, and others will be over whelmed like Fate.<br /> <br /> Being about to see things as they are and understand them isn't as helpful them those things don't make any sense as far as the Mage is concerned.<br /> <br /> You send them to where the Strix come from, leaving them trapped there with no idea how to get out. The Lower Depths are a great place to add more horror to Mage as they are alien and don't always work like they're supposed to.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2241-omegaphallic","name":"Omegaphallic","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3435"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-23T04:17:36-07:00","id":974983,"title":"","content":"As others have mentioned, lack of knowledge isn't necessary for horror and can sometimes even be detrimental. A monster can be much more horrifying if the target knows that it's coming for him and knows that his best tricks can't stop it. Especially for Mages, as their first reaction to a monster they know nothing about is curiosity, not fear.<br /> Knowing that a Mask can weather most anything you can throw at it doesn't make it less terrifying.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>milo v3</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/974857-where-is-the-horror-of-monsters-in-mage?p=974889#post974889\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Extempore are prometheans that are so weird that even in Azothic Memory has no idea what they are.... Mage sight doesn't even know what symbols even make up a normal promethean's power, these guys would be complete black hole when it comes to mages trying to understand them.</div> </div> </div> </div>The problem with this is that this doesn't make Extempores more interesting or terrifying to Mages, it makes them more boring. There uncountable horrors in the CofD that Mages can risk their lives and sanity to examine for traces of the Supernal, why waste time on one of the very few where they can't? In my experience player reaction to these complete black holes is to shrug, put them on their list of 'supernally unknowables' and go find something more interesting to poke.<br /> Unless of course making Extempores to Mages as Seattle is to Mummies is the goal of the whole thing. Not my preferred kind of crossover, but some just want certain splats to stay away from something related to theirs.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/158192-what-s-your-custom-legacy?p=1109906#post1109906\" target=\"_blank\">My custom legacy</a> (2e)- The Disciples of Rathma - Life/Death focused Moros/Thyrsus Legacy, comments appreciated","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/166-joker","name":"Joker","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar166_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"477"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-23T04:59:15-07:00","id":974995,"title":"","content":"I've not read all the books but there are several mage-killing monsters in them. The thing that eats up the Eleventh Question, for instance, and the Man-Eater, which has eaten Awakened mages over the centuries. That's not getting into things like the other supernaturals, who can have powers that counter mage abilities, including attempts to scry on them.<br /> <br /> Mages aren't all-knowing, all-seeing or all-powerful. Their power is great, but still limited, and putting a 'hidden' horror in the game shouldn't be too difficult.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-23T05:16:31-07:00","id":975001,"title":"","content":"Dr. Who does it every episode.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2632-mrmdubois","name":"Mrmdubois","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6827"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-23T05:22:42-07:00","id":975005,"title":"","content":"You could create a horror so unnatural and vile, that the better you can precieve, the more damage you take. So using Mage sight or Keening on this horror actual deal damage, as the very knowledge of this thing rots the flesh of those who possess it, the deeper the knowledge the deeper the rot.<br /> <br /> A point of bashing damage for ever perception roll success, two per success is it magical peception rolls like Mage Sight, Keenibg, or the enhanced senses of a Werewolf or Vampire.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":6}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2241-omegaphallic","name":"Omegaphallic","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3435"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-23T07:54:42-07:00","id":975049,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Okami</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/974857-where-is-the-horror-of-monsters-in-mage?p=974864#post974864\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Four words my friend; John Carpenter's The Thing<br /> <br /> Edit: Seven more words; Knowledge and Rationality Will Not Save You</div> </div> </div> </div>It doesn't have to be the monster hiding in literal shadows. It can also hide in other ways. Like you say, in The Thing, the monster hides not in the shadows, but in the form of the people it's killed. Or consider zombies. One zombie on its own is not a big threat. You can predict it, and kill it. A zombie horde, on the other hand, is very unpredictable because you never know how many there are, or where one will turn up next. The zombie horde is effectively hiding everywhere at once. If you have a zombie radar and a perfect map of every building you're traveling through, then zombies become much less scary.<br /> <br /> The problem here is that mages can solve these problems as well.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/191-strill","name":"Strill","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1261"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-23T08:25:09-07:00","id":975062,"title":"","content":"So play <i>to</i> that. Give them threats and mysteries that others would find impossible, but that Mages might, just <i>might</i>, be able to solve and/or deal with. And even if they roughly know what they are dealing with, that doesn't mean they can always do anything about it.<br /> <br /> Take something that snatches people into some other realm, moving along their Sympathetic Connections. Sure, the Mages can trace/track the thing's victims, and they even might eventually learn where it is taking them, but that doesn't mean they can really stop it, necessarily. Well, given the tools they <i>do</i> possess, I suppose they might be able to quarantine (and thus, write off) the thing's victims, but are they willing to take that step? What happens when it's already in their social network? Who are they prepared to lose?<br /> <br /> Or turn their own power against them: pit them against a Time Mage or something with some command of Time. Anything they do directly to neutralize it gets Retcon'd. So they need to think sideways, and set things up beyond their foe's ability or desire to rewind.<br /> <br /> Or the previous opener, more literally: they are against a doppelganger from another timeline/universe. Can they beat themselves? Even if they can, what if their Nimbus is left around at the scenes of a number of crimes, and the Guardians of the Veil start giving them impolite looks?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Malkydel: &quot;And the Machine dictated; let there be adequate illumination.&quot;<br /> Yossarian: &quot;And lo, it was optimal.&quot;","edited":{"userid":7212,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7212-vent0","user":"Vent0","date":". 09-23-2016, 11:32 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7212-vent0","name":"Vent0","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7212_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5184"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-23T08:27:56-07:00","id":975063,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Strill</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/974857-where-is-the-horror-of-monsters-in-mage?p=975049#post975049\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It doesn't have to be the monster hiding in literal shadows. It can also hide in other ways. Like you say, in The Thing, the monster hides not in the shadows, but in the form of the people it's killed. Or consider zombies. One zombie on its own is not a big threat. You can predict it, and kill it. A zombie horde, on the other hand, is very unpredictable because you never know how many there are, or where one will turn up next. The zombie horde is effectively hiding everywhere at once. If you have a zombie radar and a perfect map of every building you're traveling through, then zombies become much less scary.<br /> <br /> The problem here is that mages can solve these problems as well.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah, but that's because people generally know exactly what zombies are capable of. They're well known, and information galore exists on them, so of course they lose a bit of that scariness of the unknown type thing, at this point they represent a completely different type of horror, which is fine. But what happens when the zombies aren't doing what you expect, and exhibit powers not native to them? How much of what you know of zombies can be relied on at the point? What happens when you shoot them in the head, destroy the brain, and they keep on coming? What if you then destroy the heart, the spinal cord, hell, even their <i>liver</i> and they still just keep coming at you? And then these mindless undead (and yes, you checked to see if they were mindless using Mind and they are) start using psychic powers against you. Who even knows what other powers they're going to bust out?<br /> <br /> I mean sure, you can always observe them for extended periods of time to learn all of their tricks (at least the ones they use regularly), but that pretty much applies to any sort of unknown horror regardless of whether you're playing a Mage or not.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2309-ashenrogue","name":"Ashenrogue","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2309_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1388"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-23T08:35:57-07:00","id":975066,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ashenrogue</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/974857-where-is-the-horror-of-monsters-in-mage?p=975063#post975063\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Yeah, but that's because people generally know exactly what zombies are capable of. They're well known, and information galore exists on them, so of course they lose a bit of that scariness of the unknown type thing, at this point they represent a completely different type of horror, which is fine. But what happens when the zombies aren't doing what you expect, and exhibit powers not native to them? How much of what you know of zombies can be relied on at the point? What happens when you shoot them in the head, destroy the brain, and they keep on coming? What if you then destroy the heart, the spinal cord, hell, even their <i>liver</i> and they still just keep coming at you? And then these mindless undead (and yes, you checked to see if they were mindless using Mind and they are) start using psychic powers against you. Who even knows what other powers they're going to bust out?<br /> <br /> I mean sure, you can always observe them for extended periods of time to learn all of their tricks (at least the ones they use regularly), but that pretty much applies to any sort of unknown horror regardless of whether you're playing a Mage or not.</div> </div> </div> </div>And what happens when whatever is responsible is aware of and <i>uses</i> observation/awareness of itself to spread/attack? When Knowledge Itself is the danger?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Malkydel: &quot;And the Machine dictated; let there be adequate illumination.&quot;<br /> Yossarian: &quot;And lo, it was optimal.&quot;","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7212-vent0","name":"Vent0","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7212_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5184"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-23T08:40:06-07:00","id":975070,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ashenrogue</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/974857-where-is-the-horror-of-monsters-in-mage?p=975063#post975063\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Who even knows what other powers they're going to bust out?</div> </div> </div> </div>You do, because mage sight and/or Knowing spells tell you everything about them.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/191-strill","name":"Strill","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1261"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-23T08:53:23-07:00","id":975079,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Strill</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/974857-where-is-the-horror-of-monsters-in-mage?p=975070#post975070\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">You do, because mage sight and/or Knowing spells tell you everything about them.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's assuming you know what to look for and have the luxury of being able to safely observe the creature <i>and</i> have the arcana necessary to do so. I feel you're making a lot of pretty big assumptions.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2309-ashenrogue","name":"Ashenrogue","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2309_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1388"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-23T08:54:43-07:00","id":975081,"title":"","content":"Generally, not before you witness it. This has been said before, but an excellent way to lead Mages by the nose is to play on their preconceptions.<br /> <br /> This seems like another non-issue that's getting more attention than necessary.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2632-mrmdubois","name":"Mrmdubois","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6827"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-23T09:05:58-07:00","id":975084,"title":"","content":"It seems like boiled down to its basics, the problem is simply:<br /> <br /> Monsters are mysterious unknowns, but Mages have the ability and resources to learn about the unknown.<br /> <br /> Monsters are dangerous, but so are Mages, especially prepared ones.<br /> <br /> ...but it seems like in both of those sentences &quot;Mages&quot; could be replaced with &quot;Hunters.&quot; Mages have more tools to learn about things and more tools to fight them, but the basic shape of things is the same, and it seems straightforwardly the case that if you can menace and horrify Hunters and their players then you can do the same to Mages.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12136-jacobkosh","name":"jacobkosh","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12136_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"47"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-23T09:09:58-07:00","id":975087,"title":"","content":"The sad truth is real horror comes not from just the unknown but from a sense of helplessness, something I haven't seen any RPG outside of Cthullu achieve (or want to). Yes Mages can identify the threat, feel out its strengths and weaknesses, and then have the power to carry the fight to the enemy but then so do Vampires, Werewolves, Changelings and the monster of the week. For me the game works best when most of the story is about going through those steps and then taking out the baddie. In my experience the best monsters are ones intelligent enough to return the favor. I also recommend evil minions or multiple, yet subtly different, monsters, so your characters can take some out and enjoy the battle without derailing the entire plot in the first act (no monologue-ing).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6084-2pttakrill","name":"2ptTakrill","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"942"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-23T09:14:32-07:00","id":975090,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Strill</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/974857-where-is-the-horror-of-monsters-in-mage?p=975070#post975070\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">You do, because mage sight and/or Knowing spells tell you everything about them.</div> </div> </div> </div>There really isn't any knowing spell that tells you a Horror's Dread Powers, Slasher's undertaking or human's supernatural merits (TBF not 100% sure on that last one), for any monster you haven't seen before the only thing spells can tell you is 'you have never seen a creature of this type before', and there aren't any knowing spells to tell you what Utterances/Gifts/Disciplines etc. a monster from one of the other big-splats has available either.<br /> Knowing every power they have available is really only possible for Spirits/Goetia/Ghosts/other Mages, and even those have ways to get their hands on tricks you can't see with basic knowing spells.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/158192-what-s-your-custom-legacy?p=1109906#post1109906\" target=\"_blank\">My custom legacy</a> (2e)- The Disciples of Rathma - Life/Death focused Moros/Thyrsus Legacy, comments appreciated","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/166-joker","name":"Joker","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar166_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"477"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-23T09:28:18-07:00","id":975095,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Joker</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/974857-where-is-the-horror-of-monsters-in-mage?p=975090#post975090\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> There really isn't any knowing spell that tells you a Horror's Dread Powers </div> </div> </div> </div>Of course there are. Right there in the chapter about Creative Thaumaturgy.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1090276-2e-moinen-s-homebrew-hub\" target=\"_blank\">[2E] Moinen's Homebrew Hub</a></b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1747-moinen","name":"Moinen","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1747_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"889"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-23T10:57:50-07:00","id":975127,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>2ptTakrill</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/974857-where-is-the-horror-of-monsters-in-mage?p=975087#post975087\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The sad truth is real horror comes not from just the unknown but from a sense of helplessness, something I haven't seen any RPG outside of Cthullu achieve (or want to). Yes Mages can identify the threat, feel out its strengths and weaknesses, and then have the power to carry the fight to the enemy but then so do Vampires, Werewolves, Changelings and the monster of the week. For me the game works best when most of the story is about going through those steps and then taking out the baddie. In my experience the best monsters are ones intelligent enough to return the favor. I also recommend evil minions or multiple, yet subtly different, monsters, so your characters can take some out and enjoy the battle without derailing the entire plot in the first act (no monologue-ing).</div> </div> </div> </div>I don't know, I feel like vanilla ChroD does helplessness just as well as CoC. And the other lines do a good job of giving the protagonists a niche and supplying out-of-context threats.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-23T11:10:21-07:00","id":975132,"title":"","content":"On the issue of helplessness, while a vampire or werewolf aren't likely to be bothered much by an individual human, a mage can still just be shot in the back of the head or hit by a car when unexpected. This is obviously not a building-up of dread situation, but it could be the climax of a series of threats or insidious warnings about something bad going to happen.<br /> <br /> However there's a lot more in the universe than just humans. And even if a mage was able to recognize a threat, find out the individual supernatural being that might cause it, and find out its weaknesses, and even gather them up, keep in mind that's a lot of steps and not something a mage is guaranteed to be able to do, they still would have to use said weaknesses against the being. As it is ephemerals of Rank 5 have weird banes that are not going to be easy to reproduce, and in the game of mage, the concept of dealing with even higher Rank creatures is a possibility.<br /> <br /> Yes, you can have a game where a mid-to-high 'level' mage punches out Cthulhu. But in the larger picture of things, Cthulhu is a high priest of Azathoth, who mages won't be able to punch out, and who is likely to be the kind of threat a high 'level' mage will deal with.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Moinen</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/974857-where-is-the-horror-of-monsters-in-mage?p=975095#post975095\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Of course there are. Right there in the chapter about Creative Thaumaturgy.</div> </div> </div> </div>Could you give me a page number?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":886,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","user":"nofather","date":". 09-23-2016, 02:16 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-23T11:21:09-07:00","id":975137,"title":"","content":"It's Step Three on p.125<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <b>The effects of a spell can be incredibly broad, and it\u2019s impossible<br /> to categorize every conceivable thing a mage might want</b><br /> <b>to do with a spell</b>, but this section will highlight some of the<br /> more common effects, how to adjudicate them, and what they<br /> should cost. </div> </div> </div>Dread Powers are nothing exeptional so when some Freddy Krugger comes in your dream and you happen to be a Mastigos you just do some Knowing and Unveiling.<br /> It's vampire? Just port rules of Know Spirit into your brand new Know Dead spell.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1090276-2e-moinen-s-homebrew-hub\" target=\"_blank\">[2E] Moinen's Homebrew Hub</a></b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1747-moinen","name":"Moinen","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1747_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"889"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-23T11:40:59-07:00","id":975145,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Moinen</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/974857-where-is-the-horror-of-monsters-in-mage?p=975137#post975137\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Dread Powers are nothing exeptional so when some Freddy Krugger comes in your dream and you happen to be a Mastigos you just do some Knowing and Unveiling.<br /> It's vampire? Just port rules of Know Spirit into your brand new Know Dead spell.</div> </div> </div> </div>Can you cast spells in your dreams? Even if you can, suppose you see Freddy Krueger in your dreams. At some point during the fight you realize, hey, my Mind spells aren't quite working on him and start throwing Death spells at him, including Know Dead. You manage to survive the thing terrorizing and assaulting you in your dreams, and put it together. He's powered by your fear. Simple Mind spell, you are a Mastigos, you quell your fears and he is powerless in your dreams, fading away to nothing.<br /> <br /> You wake up the next morning with a few Bashing wounds, laying beside your girlfriend's body, as she's been clawed to death and has an expression of absolute fear on her face.<br /> <br /> Is this not a 'horror of monsters' situation?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-23T11:48:56-07:00","id":975148,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Moinen</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/974857-where-is-the-horror-of-monsters-in-mage?p=975137#post975137\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It's Step Three on p.125<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> Dread Powers are nothing exeptional so when some Freddy Krugger comes in your dream and you happen to be a Mastigos you just do some Knowing and Unveiling.<br /> It's vampire? Just port rules of Know Spirit into your brand new Know Dead spell.</div> </div> </div> </div>Which is also all subject to ST approval. And personally I wouldn't allow a spell that tells you literally everything about a vampire and their abilities from just having Death, because they and their abilities are more than just that. Oh you'd get some information, definitely, but it wouldn't be nearly as clear cut as something like Know Spirit, because while there is a Spirit arcanum, there's no such thing as a Vampire arcanum.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2309-ashenrogue","name":"Ashenrogue","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2309_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1388"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-23T11:52:57-07:00","id":975150,"title":"","content":"I think an important part of being a Mage GM is unlearning the reflex to try and be cute with information. Instead of scouring the rules looking for exceptions to Knowing spells or devising ever-cleverer schemes to withhold information, time is ultimately better spent accepting that Awakened characters will uncover the truth, or at least the bullet points, and focusing on what they do with the knowledge.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":7}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12136-jacobkosh","name":"jacobkosh","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12136_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"47"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-23T12:19:53-07:00","id":975163,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar886_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"886\" data-vbnamecard=\"886\">nofather</a> Yes it is. Excellent example of going around the All Knowing Mages.<br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2309-ashenrogue\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar2309_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"2309\" data-vbnamecard=\"2309\">Ashenrogue</a> Even when an Obrimos checks someone's nature with a spell I dont tell them that the someone is a vampire or a mage. I tell them that the sense deep hunger or ever present curiosity. Moros casts Know Dead? Great! He senses Celerity as a haze and wind in his hair, Auspex gives a sens of someone looking back at you. Mages know things. Just make this knowledge interesting.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1090276-2e-moinen-s-homebrew-hub\" target=\"_blank\">[2E] Moinen's Homebrew Hub</a></b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1747-moinen","name":"Moinen","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1747_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"889"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-23T13:30:10-07:00","id":975205,"title":"","content":"I do exactly as Moinen. I tell them what they see but it's up to the players to interpret that data. Not witholding anything but at the same time not going &quot;oh he's a 3 BP vamp with 2 dots of celerity&quot; as that's quite boring. Instead I'd go &quot;The person is definately dead despite standing here and talking you. (If Life was used) You see fragments of other peoples lives flowing through his body. The way he moves seems suggests that he could stab you in a split second and you wouldn't notice&quot;","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2636-menace","name":"Menace","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2636_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"527"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-23T13:49:03-07:00","id":975217,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Menace</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/974857-where-is-the-horror-of-monsters-in-mage?p=975205#post975205\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I do exactly as Moinen. I tell them what they see but it's up to the players to interpret that data. Not witholding anything but at the same time not going &quot;oh he's a 3 BP vamp with 2 dots of celerity&quot; as that's quite boring. Instead I'd go &quot;The person is definately dead despite standing here and talking you. (If Life was used) You see fragments of other peoples lives flowing through his body. The way he moves seems suggests that he could stab you in a split second and you wouldn't notice&quot;</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Yes, this is part of the solution, and the book tells us explicitly to do this as storytellers, in the side bar &quot;Theory, Practice, and Crossover&quot; on page 91 of the Mage: The Awakening 2nd Edition.<br /> <br /> To venture away from the book, if you want unknowable things, make it so. There are questions which don't have answers. There are questions that have answers that would take longer than the time of the universe to calculate. There are known unknowns, and unknown unknowns. Mage's get to exist above us when it comes to this. However, if there are supernal truths, I don't know why there couldn't be a supernal version of these facts. <br /> <br /> So to move onto making a monster, I would choose a Bound. I would make it operate by accessing supernal unknowns.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":8619,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8619-aforgeforthoughts","user":"AForgeForThoughts","date":". 09-23-2016, 04:53 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8619-aforgeforthoughts","name":"AForgeForThoughts","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"60"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-23T16:45:09-07:00","id":975275,"title":"","content":"I'm currently running a game, and the main way I'm maintaining horror for it is twofold:<br /> <br /> First, learning that something is there that nobody else can detect. That blood-soaked carpet upstairs was made as a result of actions from a thing that is still there, not some mass murderer. Not something nearly so trite. You have some idea of what it could be, but then again, there's a dozen things that fit that bill. But you're the only one in the house now. <br /> <br /> Secondly, knowledge does not guarantee respite. You find the thing that caused the mass murder is still there, watching you with insectoid eyes. And once you see it it begins talking to you. You cast your spells and learn just how threatening it is. How absolutely certain it is that if the monster is left unchecked it will tear a hole in reality and usher in something worse. You see just how many dice it will roll, how badly it'll hurt you. So you try to find another way to deal with it. And you do. You learn human sacrifice is the only way it'll go for good. Or so you think. Maybe there's another way? Maybe you overlooked something?<br /> <br /> And now you have a moral dilemma. And you let the PCs feel it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12121-ainsoph","name":"AinSoph","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"35"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-23T17:25:33-07:00","id":975297,"title":"","content":"There's a man they call The Lover. Well, sometimes a man. He steps into people's lives. He takes their faces, steals their histories, assumes their identities. The originals are always found faceless, if they're found at all. How he takes their memories is unknown. <i>Why </i>he does it is unknown. And when he's wearing the face of a loved one, he's almost impossible to detect. I know; I've been hunting him for eight years. He took my fiance's face and used it to take my innocence, my sense of place, and security. How do you track someone who can appear as anyone, and has the skills gleaned from countless stolen memories? For all I know, <i>you </i>could be him. For all <i>you </i>know, <i>I </i>could be him....<br /> <br /> Bones ain't s'posed to walk. Especially when you dig 'em up out of the river. But I seen 'em, that poor bastard they dredged up. Down there for years. No one knows how long, really. I mean, he ain't talkin'. Just walkin'. Every night he gets up out of the evidence storage, he goes on down to the river, and he paces up and down the shore, see. I don't know what he's lookin' for and I ain't about to ask him. Buddy did that once, heard this thing's voice, and now all he can dream about is drownin'. Things whisperin' to him in the dark. Dead things. Down in the bottom o' that river. I did some research. Passenger boat went down in that real harsh winter in the '30s. Near three dozen died. The crazy thing is, I think he's lookin' for the others. As far as strange things go, that makes sense enough to me. What I worry about is ... what's he gonna do when he <i>finds </i>'em?<br /> <br /> I study cryptids. The world is full of them, many more than even an archmaster could catalogue. Not every cryptid is a Jersey Devil, a Bigfoot, a remnant of the past. Some things have no direct analogues in the world we know. Some are older than human civilization. See this one here, I only managed to capture a low-quality photo. Looks like it could be a whale, right? It's not. <i>That's just its tail. </i>It's huge. Unimaginably huge. And from what I've been able to tell, it wasn't young even back when the continents are all merged. It eats everything it can, and every time there's a sighting, for the next few weeks, strange things appear in the water, wash up on shore. I saw one. It didn't last long, the wretched thing, but it was ... frightening, and fascinating, all at once. Lower body of a man, upper body of an echinoderm, and that voice.... *shudders* See, I think this thing is trying to create life. It's what I've gathered for years. It creates living things out of all these pieces of creatures and objects and everything it eats. What it does with them inside its gut to make them this way, or what it's trying to create, I don't know. But if my estimations are correct, it's the largest aquatic predator to have ever existed. It's millions of years old. And it <i>also eats spirits</i>. It seems to be able to travel between this realm and the next, and if it does finally succeed at whatever it's doing, what if it creates another one like itself?<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Freelancer </b>\u2022 Available for work!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/109-claire-redfield","name":"Claire Redfield","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar109_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"672"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-23T18:56:59-07:00","id":975327,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>jacobkosh</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/974857-where-is-the-horror-of-monsters-in-mage?p=975084#post975084\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It seems like boiled down to its basics, the problem is simply:<br /> <br /> Monsters are mysterious unknowns, but Mages have the ability and resources to learn about the unknown.<br /> <br /> Monsters are dangerous, but so are Mages, especially prepared ones.<br /> <br /> ...but it seems like in both of those sentences &quot;Mages&quot; could be replaced with &quot;Hunters.&quot; Mages have more tools to learn about things and more tools to fight them, but the basic shape of things is the same, and it seems straightforwardly the case that if you can menace and horrify Hunters and their players then you can do the same to Mages.</div> </div> </div> </div>I don't agree with the second one. You can be dangerous, but still be frightened by a monster. Look at Arnold Schwarzenegger vs the Predator. The second one is more accurately described as &quot;Monsters are dangerous, but mages can become invulnerable&quot;. Very very few things can harm living shadow.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/191-strill","name":"Strill","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1261"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-23T19:04:49-07:00","id":975329,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Strill</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/974857-where-is-the-horror-of-monsters-in-mage?p=975327#post975327\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The second one is more accurately described as &quot;Monsters are dangerous, but mages can become invulnerable&quot;. Very very few things can harm living shadow.</div> </div> </div> </div>Very few mundane things. Other things can harm, capture, absorb or otherwise disassemble them.<br /> <br /> What kind of monsters do you think mages deal with that are so impotent?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-23T19:56:43-07:00","id":975345,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>nofather</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/974857-where-is-the-horror-of-monsters-in-mage?p=975329#post975329\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Very few mundane things. Other things can harm, capture, absorb or otherwise disassemble them.<br /> <br /> What kind of monsters do you think mages deal with that are so impotent?</div> </div> </div> </div>Spirits, Ghosts, Goetia. What are they going to do against living shadow, unless they have shadow as an influence?<br /> <br /> You can make the same argument for shifting into Twilight/Shadow. Yeah, you might encounter some monsters that can shift into those states, but it stretches disbelief when nearly every monster can shift into twilight and shadow.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/191-strill","name":"Strill","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1261"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-23T20:02:51-07:00","id":975348,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Strill</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/974857-where-is-the-horror-of-monsters-in-mage?p=975345#post975345\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Spirits, Ghosts, Goetia. What are they going to do against living shadow, unless they have shadow as an influence?<br /> <br /> You can make the same argument for shifting into Twilight/Shadow. Yeah, you might encounter some monsters that can shift into those states, but it stretches disbelief when nearly every monster can shift into twilight and shadow.</div> </div> </div> </div>Pretty much anything that an ST feels being a shadow wont protect you from. Which, IMO is a lot. Mind fuckery would be fair game at a lot of tables. While it mentions Supernal magic being an exception I could see a lot of ST's also equating that to the powers of most of the big supernaturals as well, so Disciplines, Gifts, etc., that make sense.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2309-ashenrogue","name":"Ashenrogue","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2309_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1388"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-23T20:07:57-07:00","id":975350,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Strill</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/974857-where-is-the-horror-of-monsters-in-mage?p=975345#post975345\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Spirits, Ghosts, Goetia. What are they going to do against living shadow, unless they have shadow as an influence?</div> </div> </div> </div>Turn on a light? Start a fire? Use their Influence to affect your mind, which is still your mind even if you're shadow. Your sample mage has a specific ability, why wouldn't the antagonist? And incidentally there's also Abyssal and Supernal beings. Or perhaps the mage whose brain they've eaten and now completely control can just dispel said living shadow ability. There's probably an infinite amount of ways to develop a threat, and that's just something that can threaten a mage who is already on the defensive. Turning into living shadow is not an offensive power that's going to defeat a monster.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> You can make the same argument for shifting into Twilight/Shadow. Yeah, you might encounter some monsters that can shift into those states, but it stretches disbelief when nearly every monster can shift into twilight and shadow. </div> </div> </div>This sort of argument might work if this was a MMO where a mage could simply blow through pre-made areas that a vampire couldn't, but it's not. The storyteller isn't playing blind, they know they're running mage, and they know the capabilities of the player characters. It's not 'stretching disbelief' to have them dealing with antagonists that are capable on their own.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-23T20:53:40-07:00","id":975360,"title":"","content":"One of my favorite characters was a Mage who had a toxic association with a lust demon (partly a Goetic thing partly homebrewed). Sure he knew what it was and that it was bad for him, sure he could magic it to death if he needed to, but that wouldn't change the fact that it had its hooks in him because of his own faults. It didn't matter how powerful he was, as long as he lacked the strength to overcome his addictions this monkey would always be on his back, the demon just being a personified form of it. That futility was pretty horrifying.<br /> <br /> There's also the fact that monsters don't have to hurt you specifically to be threatening or horrifying. You may be able to slip into Twilight safely out of danger, but unless your loved ones can too you aren't out of the woods. <br /> <br /> Easily my favorite episode of the new Doctor Who is &quot;Midnight.&quot; Because it features a creature whom the Doctor can't beat with a convenient epiphany. He's never seen anything like it, he doesn't know what it is, it almost kills him save for an ordinary person throwing themselves in the way, and at the end he still doesn't know anything more about it. It clearly terrifies him to his core and undermining the certainty that character always carries makes that episode sing.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2507,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2507-caitiff-primogen","user":"Caitiff Primogen","date":". 09-23-2016, 11:59 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2507-caitiff-primogen","name":"Caitiff Primogen","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2507_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1351"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-23T22:10:53-07:00","id":975374,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>nofather</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/974857-where-is-the-horror-of-monsters-in-mage?p=975350#post975350\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Turn on a light? Start a fire? Use their Influence to affect your mind, which is still your mind even if you're shadow. Your sample mage has a specific ability, why wouldn't the antagonist? And incidentally there's also Abyssal and Supernal beings. Or perhaps the mage whose brain they've eaten and now completely control can just dispel said living shadow ability. There's probably an infinite amount of ways to develop a threat, and that's just something that can threaten a mage who is already on the defensive. Turning into living shadow is not an offensive power that's going to defeat a monster.</div> </div> </div> </div>Why would lights or fire hurt her? The spell says that you're invisible if you're already in a dark area, implying that you can be in a light area. It says nothing about taking damage from light, so I don't see why light or fire would accomplish anything.<br /> <br /> Yes, you're vulnerable to psychic attacks. Yes Supernal and Abyssal beings are able to counter most defenses a mage can throw up. No, stealing a mage's body wouldn't give you access to their power. A mage's power is controlled by their will alone. It's not possible to control it in any other way. If you're invulnerable you can just sit there and cast fraying or unraveling spells on the monster until it dies.<br /> <br /> So the bottom line here is that in order to harm a mage who is living shadow, you need a monster that can target either the mind, the soul, or who has supernal power of its own. That's a very limited array of enemies, especially since even an enemy who can target the mind or soul would still be countered by shifting into Twilight.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> This sort of argument might work if this was a MMO where a mage could simply blow through pre-made areas that a vampire couldn't, but it's not. The storyteller isn't playing blind, they know they're running mage, and they know the capabilities of the player characters. It's not 'stretching disbelief' to have them dealing with antagonists that are capable on their own. </div> </div> </div>You're essentially saying that the storyteller should specifically hand-tailor enemies to hard-counter the player's abilities. That sounds more like petty passive-aggressive revenge than an interesting story. Furthermore, it's a perfect example of the problem I gave in the OP. If you can't have threatening monsters without them having some highly specific way to undermine the mage, then you can't make threatening monsters in general.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>CaitiffPrimogen</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">There's also the fact that monsters don't have to hurt you specifically to be threatening or horrifying. You may be able to slip into Twilight safely out of danger, but unless your loved ones can too you aren't out of the woods.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's why after you become invulnerable, you cast unraveling spells on it until it dies. Or you turn it into a sheep and slit its throat. Or any number of other ways to kill it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":191,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/191-strill","user":"Strill","date":". 09-24-2016, 01:13 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/191-strill","name":"Strill","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1261"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-23T22:27:27-07:00","id":975377,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Strill</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/974857-where-is-the-horror-of-monsters-in-mage?p=975374#post975374\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Why would lights or fire hurt her? The spell says that you're invisible if you're already in a dark area, implying that you can be in a light area. It says nothing about taking damage from light, so I don't see why light or fire would accomplish anything.</div> </div> </div> </div>Eh, I looked up the spell halfway through my post. But lights would certainly allow you to see the shadow-mage and keep track of them.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Yes, you're vulnerable to psychic attacks. Yes Supernal and Abyssal beings are able to counter most defenses a mage can throw up. No, stealing a mage's body wouldn't give you access to their power. A mage's power is controlled by their will alone. It's not possible to control it in any other way. If you're invulnerable you can just sit there and cast fraying or unraveling spells on the monster until it dies. </div> </div> </div>That's not quite true. Said brain-eater could have simply co-opted their brain, similar to the Cloud Infinite. And yes, if you are invulnerable you can just sit there and cast fraying or unraveling spells, but none of these options you've suggested make a mage completely invulnerable.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> So the bottom line here is that in order to harm a mage who is living shadow, you need a monster that can target either the mind, the soul, or who has supernal power of its own. That's a very limited array of enemies, especially since even an enemy who can target the mind or soul would still be countered by shifting into Twilight. </div> </div> </div>It's not very limited. All Goetia, many spirits, various ghosts or angels, all Supernal and Abyssals, mages, other supernaturals. Considering just a few posts ago you thought mages would only encounter spirits, Goetia and ghosts, that's still about half of them. I'm imagining you also know that Dread Powers and Numina are not limited to the examples in the books, that through the Storyteller they are as infinite, more even, than the abilities of a mage with all the arcana they can get.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> You're essentially saying that the storyteller should specifically hand-tailor enemies to hard-counter the player's abilities. That sounds more like petty passive-aggressive revenge than an interesting story. Furthermore, it's a perfect example of the problem I gave in the OP. If you can't have threatening monsters without them having some highly specific way to undermine the mage, then you can't make threatening monsters in general. </div> </div> </div>No. I'm saying the storyteller should specifically hand-tailor enemies to be a <i>challenge </i>to the player character abilities. We've had a number of examples of how this could be done in this thread.<br /> <br /> This is the same thing you do in every game, mage is not special in that regard. When running vampire, your only options are not nocturnal dogs or the Sun God. When running werewolf, you are not limited to having the antagonists be John Goodman or Father Wolf.<br /> <br /> You are suggesting that there is no challenge or fear. That there can only be easily-defeated antagonists, or undefeatable 'hard-counter' antagonists. And despite evidence to the contrary, you keep insisting this is the only way things can happen.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":886,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","user":"nofather","date":". 09-24-2016, 01:38 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-23T22:47:50-07:00","id":975381,"title":"","content":"Heh. Locked in perspective. That's funny in context.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kelly R.S. Steele, Freelance Writer(Feel free to call me Kelly, Arcane, or Arc)</b><br /> The world is not beautiful, therefore it is.-Keiichi Sigsawa, Kino's Journey<br /> Feminine pronouns, please.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","name":"ArcaneArts","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar99_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11384"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-23T22:50:29-07:00","id":975382,"title":"","content":"Want Monster Horror in <b>Mage</b>? Just use Abyssal Beings. Take your Abyss creature, use some of our <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/925972-2e-abyssal-aspects-and-numina\">Abyssal Aspects</a>, and you have<i> really</i> scary monster for Awakened.<br /> <br /> <i>Literally, </i>on our last London game scared my cabal of Masters with nice little Abyssal Being like that. So it's more than doable. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/cool.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Cool\" smilieid=\"6\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1437-wyrdhamster","name":"wyrdhamster","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1437_5.jpg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11313"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-23T23:12:58-07:00","id":975389,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>nofather</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/974857-where-is-the-horror-of-monsters-in-mage?p=975377#post975377\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">That's not quite true. Said brain-eater could have simply co-opted their brain, similar to the Cloud Infinite. And yes, if you are invulnerable you can just sit there and cast fraying or unraveling spells, but none of these options you've suggested make a mage completely invulnerable.</div> </div> </div> </div>You can't be completely invulnerable to everything, and it doesn't matter whether you are. All that matters is whether you're invulnerable to everything your target can do to hurt you.<br /> <br /> Cloud Infinite still can't force a mage to cast a spell.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> It's not very limited. All Goetia, many spirits, various angels, all Supernal and Abyssals, mages, other supernaturals. </div> </div> </div>It's not enough to have a psychic power. You also have to know what you're targeting, and have a power that can make a mage give up. Plenty of spirits or goetia can make you angry, or want to do drugs or something, but that's not going to accomplish anything useful in this situation. Most importantly, they still can't injure you without a very specific numina or influence that precisely counters your spell. Also, vampires are out because they have to make eye contact.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Considering just a few posts ago you thought mages would only encounter spirits, Goetia and angels </div> </div> </div>I said no such thing. Someone asked me to give examples and so I gave them. Also, I said ghosts, not angels.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> , that's still about half of them. I'm imagining you also know that Dread Powers and Numina are not limited to the examples in the books, that through the Storyteller they are as infinite, more even, than the abilities of a mage with all the arcana they can get. </div> </div> </div>A player with Death 4 can shift into ghost twilight, and turn into living shadow. Spirits can't see him. Goetia can't see him. Most supernal/abyssal beings can't see him. Most mages can't see him. Vampires can't see him. Werewolves can't see him. Changelings can't see him. Prometheans can't see him. Any other monster that's not some kind of ghost can't see him. Anything that IS a ghost still might not be able to see him if he's in a patch of darkness, still can't physically hurt him, and needs specific mental or soul-based numina to even so much as interact with him.<br /> <br /> That's way too specific to build your campaign around.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> No. I'm saying the storyteller should specifically hand-tailor enemies to be a <i>challenge </i>to the player character abilities. We've had a number of examples of how this could be done in this thread.<br /> <br /> You are suggesting that there is no challenge or fear. That there can only be easily-defeated antagonists, or undefeatable 'hard-counter' antagonists. And despite evidence to the contrary, you keep insisting this is the only way things can happen. </div> </div> </div>And because the player's abilities are so powerful, the only way to <i>challenge</i> them is to hard-counter their defenses.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/191-strill","name":"Strill","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1261"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-23T23:33:47-07:00","id":975393,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Strill</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/974857-where-is-the-horror-of-monsters-in-mage?p=975389#post975389\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It's not enough to have a psychic power. You also have to know what you're targeting, and have a power that can make a mage give up. Plenty of spirits or goetia can make you angry, or want to do drugs or something, but that's not going to accomplish anything useful in this situation. Most importantly, they still can't injure you without a very specific numina or influence that precisely counters your spell. Also, vampires are out because they have to make eye contact.</div> </div> </div> </div>You have to see the target, but only mages I believe really need to know what they're targeting, and not entirely with them. Eye contact is necessary only for Dominate, and possibly Face of the Beast, a Nightmare power. One could just use Majesty to lure the mage back into physicality. Regarding the ephemerals, for instance with Anger you could invoke the Berserk Condition, which makes it significantly harder for a mage to cast spells.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> A player with Death 4 can shift into ghost twilight, and turn into living shadow. Spirits can't see him. Goetia can't see him. Most supernal/abyssal beings can't see him. Most mages can't see him. Vampires can't see him. Werewolves can't see him. Changelings can't see him. Prometheans can't see him. Any other monster that's not some kind of ghost can't see him. Anything that IS a ghost still might not be able to see him if he's in a patch of darkness, still can't physically hurt him, and needs specific mental or soul-based numina to even so much as interact with him. </div> </div> </div>A character can, otherwise this book would be selling a lot more. Death-attuned ephemerals can see him, this includes death spirits, and there is a nice ability that allows werewolves to see such beings. But we're back to this. Your character is hiding. They aren't hiding out of bravery, are they? They're hiding because they're scared of whatever they're facing.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> That's way too specific to build your campaign around.<br /> <br /> And because the player's abilities are so powerful, the only way to <i>challenge</i> them is to hard-counter their defenses. </div> </div> </div>It really isn't. Your necromancer is dealing with this problem for a reason, it's something <i>they </i>are interested in, it's something involved with them and is likely to have abilities in line with theirs, otherwise how did they get to this point? If not, and said necromancer is for some reason investigating a lightning demon, now that the necromancer has run away, a lightning demon can sizzle off and continue zapping people in peace, because running away doesn't defeat antagonists. Or alternatively we go back to the Freddy Krueger situation, where it doesn't matter if you turn into a shadow and slip into Twilight, because it's already done its damage while you were asleep. You're going to have to track them down through the clues in the dead they've left behind.<br /> <br /> You referred to 'good horror movies' in your original post. You know these do not start with the main character in a room with the monster and about to end it. Unless it's about to start a flashback 'how did we get here' sort of story. There's a lead-up. There's a reason a character finds themselves in that position. There's build-up, 'rising action.' When the monster is hidden in the shadows, it doesn't stick around waiting to be identified. It's hidden in the shadows for reasons, and for other reasons the protagonist finds themselves poking at those shadows. For instance in the movie Cloverfield, which popped up in a search for monster movies. The monster isn't seen fully at first, only the devastation it brings as the characters try to find a way to escape and survive. They see the smaller creatures that spawn from it, infecting others. Knowing what the monster looks like does not change the need to survive, to run from angry citizens or protect yourself from weird bugs. And yes, you won't be hurt if you turn into a shadow or shift into Twilight, but you'll be watching it all happen around you. Skipping over Pacific Rim, you have Descent. The horror there isn't just from the morlock-like creatures. Their sudden appearance is certainly a fright, but the rest of the terror involves the cramped conditions of the location, their knowledge and experience hunting in the caves compared to the protagonists, who are lost, surrounded and outnumbered, and beset by problems within the group.<br /> <br /> Again, these are things one does for all the games. These are things writers have done throughout fiction, in all manner of media. Running mage is no different. You, the storyteller, are not running blind. You should know what would count as obstacles for the player characters. If you choose not to use them, that is not the game's fault.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":886,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","user":"nofather","date":". 09-24-2016, 02:56 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-23T23:53:34-07:00","id":975395,"title":"","content":"One must be careful to not fall into the common trap when discussing Mage where one assumes that one mage can do everything in the book. Even assuming that the average group is going to have Arcana at 4 or higher is a stretch. Advancement isn't very fast in these games.<br /> <br /> Creative Thaumaturgy is also pretty restricted when it comes to supernatural things that aren't Supernal. It's limited to both Practices and Purviews. Knowing can't really give you information on supernatural powers of any creature that isn't under an appropriate Purview. So basically just Goetia, Ghosts, and Spirits. Or, more accurately, the book leaves it up to the Storyteller as to whether it can or can't. But even given that, most Horrors don't fall neatly into any particular Arcana.<br /> <br /> So in many ways, this isn't quite the issue that the OP suggests that it is.<br /> <br /> However, any group of PCs that manages to cover all or most of the Arcana at two dots (pretty easy when you can start with 2 dots in 3 Arcana; it doesn't really fall into the trap my first paragraph addresses) can basically make themselves effectively (barring Clash of Wills) untouchable with Shielding spells. And untouchable people are fairly immune to external Horror like this thread talks about.<br /> <br /> And hiding really has nothing to do with being scared, especially when you can magically tear the target apart while you do it. Snipers and assassins and predatory monsters aren't cowards. They're just being effective.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1692-maina","name":"Maina","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1692_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"724"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-23T23:56:15-07:00","id":975398,"title":"","content":"If the mages are effectively invulnerable, take a page from &quot;For the Man who has Everything.&quot; No mage can protect everyone and everything she cares about. Put them in a position where they must spread their resources thin trying to protect what's theirs, and then hit them with the horror of just what depths they'll go to in order to do it.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Freelancer </b>\u2022 Available for work!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/109-claire-redfield","name":"Claire Redfield","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar109_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"672"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-24T00:03:38-07:00","id":975401,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>nofather</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/974857-where-is-the-horror-of-monsters-in-mage?p=975393#post975393\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Eye contact is necessary only for Dominate, and possibly Face of the Beast, a Nightmare power. One could just use Majesty to lure the mage back into physicality. Regarding the ephemerals, for instance with Anger you could invoke the Berserk Condition, which makes it significantly harder for a mage to cast spells.</div> </div> </div> </div>&quot;Significantly harder&quot; doesn't mean much when time is on your side. It only takes one to three spells to kill most anything.<br /> <br /> Majesty doesn't work on a living shadow because Majesty functions on &quot;animal magnetism&quot;. A living shadow has no instincts or urges to manipulate.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> A character can, otherwise this book would be selling a lot more. Death-attuned ephemerals can see him, this includes death spirits, and there is a nice ability that allows werewolves to see such beings. But we're back to this. Your character is hiding. They aren't hiding out of bravery, are they? They're hiding because they're scared of whatever they're facing. </div> </div> </div>They're protecting themselves because they're rational. What, is a knight a coward for wearing armor?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> It really isn't. Your necromancer is dealing with this problem for a reason, it's something <i>they </i>are interested in, it's something involved with them and is likely to have abilities in line with theirs, otherwise how did they get to this white room? </div> </div> </div>So you're saying that whenever a mage gets involved with something, it has to be something that they specialize in? What nonsense is that? Maybe their cabalmate is in trouble. Maybe their mentor asked them to look into it. Maybe their order or the hierarch asked for a favor. Maybe the cabal is investigating something as a group. Maybe it looked like a ghost at first but turned out not to be. Maybe they didn't have any idea what it was. Maybe they were just walking down the street and saw something weird.<br /> <br /> You're saying that a mage's world is so neat and organized that they can just know in advance everything they're going to investigate? What would be the point of investigating if they already knew everything to begin with?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> If not, and said necromancer is for some reason investigating a lightning demon, now that the necromancer has run away, a lightning demon can sizzle off and continue zapping people in peace, because running away doesn't defeat antagonists. Or alternatively we go back to the Freddy Krueger situation, where it doesn't matter if you turn into a shadow and slip into Twilight, because it's already done its damage while you were asleep. You're going to have to track them down through the clues in the dead they've left behind. </div> </div> </div>Where are you getting the idea that anyone is running away? Shifting into twilight does not involve running away. You can still see into the physical. You can still obliterate things with unraveling spells. Your lightning demon is just going to die after the mage follows him around for a few rounds casting Withering with impunity.<br /> <br /> Even if you need to seek out and confront an enemy, it doesn't make your invulnerability any less relevant. Death spells still work in the Astral. When your astral self tracks down Freddy Kreuger, you'll still be just as invincible as ever, unless the storyteller gives him some way to specifically counter your spells.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/191-strill","name":"Strill","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1261"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-24T05:02:27-07:00","id":975473,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/191-strill\" style=\"background-image:url('data:image/svg+xml;base64,');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"191\" data-vbnamecard=\"191\">Strill</a> the whole premise of the thread is flawed, you're essentially running a white room scenario without dice. You're selectively ignoring the good rational arguments for why your concern is dumb and going after the easy targets too.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2632-mrmdubois","name":"Mrmdubois","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6827"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-24T06:18:49-07:00","id":975485,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Strill</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/974857-where-is-the-horror-of-monsters-in-mage?p=975374#post975374\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">You're essentially saying that the storyteller should specifically hand-tailor enemies to hard-counter the player's abilities. That sounds more like petty passive-aggressive revenge than an interesting story. Furthermore, it's a perfect example of the problem I gave in the OP. If you can't have threatening monsters without them having some highly specific way to undermine the mage, then you can't make threatening monsters in general.<br /> <br /> That's why after you become invulnerable, you cast unraveling spells on it until it dies. Or you turn it into a sheep and slit its throat. Or any number of other ways to kill it.</div> </div> </div> </div>Finding a balance between challenging the PCs and bulldozing them with GM fiat is a struggle GMs have faced pretty much from the beginning of RPGs. You seem to be saying its impossible, and yet the fact that people are still playing Mage: the Awakening would seem to suggest strongly that it is not.<br /> <br /> You're also missing my point. When the monster realizes it can't hurt you its probably not going to keep flailing at you like an idiot until it dies. Even if you kill it before it escapes, there are probably more of them out there who now know attacking you directly doesn't work. So they'll get creative, probably by hurting people you care about that don't have the same amount of magical protection. <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2507-caitiff-primogen","name":"Caitiff Primogen","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2507_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1351"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-24T10:51:52-07:00","id":975600,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Strill</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/974857-where-is-the-horror-of-monsters-in-mage?p=975401#post975401\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> Majesty doesn't work on a living shadow because Majesty functions on &quot;animal magnetism&quot;. A living shadow has no instincts or urges to manipulate.</div> </div> </div> </div>Hmm. Interesting. I would definitely not rule that way on Majesty- but this is actually a situation that came up directly at our table: a mage trying to defend against an onslaught from a vamp. The mage should have cake-walked it. Brief stats.<br /> <br /> Mage: death 4, life 3, mat 1, gnosis 3.<br /> Active Spells for scene: 1 Indefinite Ghost Shield (which I ruled would give a clash of wills against disciplines; it was a mage game, after all. Would not always rule this way. As indefinitely up, would offer +4 in a clash of wills.)<br /> 1 Potency six Alter Life on self (giving +6 bonuses to stealth/camoflage and all relevant senses, and in another fairly generous ruling, to initiative.)<br /> Shadow Form - or whatever that was called - that turns her into invulnerable shadow.<br /> <br /> She had, for various reasons, kidnapped a young ghoul (mage custody battle - whoah). The vampire coming in had the following stats.<br /> Obfuscate 3.<br /> Brawl 2, Stealth 2, strength 2, BP3.<br /> I ghoul retainer with Obfuscate 3.<br /> <br /> Now, this is how it fell out.<br /> The mage expected a backlash - so at night hung out at her sanctum protected as above.<br /> In comes the Obfuscated Ghoul first. Mage wins clash of wills easy, blasts them from shadow form, taking 1 Pdox, but whatever. Body of the ghoul is now lying outside sanctum, Obfuscate broken.<br /> Passer by notices it, calls an ambulance. Mage, quick thinking, changes into human form and comes out to try to move the body.<br /> At which point, late arriving vampire sees mage. Narrowly misses Obfuscate clash of wills, but massively wins a mundane Stealth check. Attacks, goes into frenzy near dead ghoul, and entirely drains the mage in two rounds. She stays conscious due to death armour, but with 2 health left, gives in, and feeling generous, I let her wake at the start of next session in a garage somewhere, drugged and with an IV drip in her arm of Mekhet vitae.<br /> <br /> But here's the thing: yes, we assumed the battle would be easy; with so much health (through nuDeath armour) and an easy spell to slip back into shadow form should it all go down. But even with weak stats, with blood pumping and willpower and frenzy, she was lucky enough to escape. We rerolled the battle afterwards a few times, but it kept coming up similar; either the mage put the vamp down quickly, or if the vamp got his hands on her - it was over pretty soon. Perhaps she shouldn't have left shadow form; might have gone down very differently, but she would have had to leave it at some point; and the way things fell out, she played the hand dealt her.<br /> <br /> I don't know. Yes - a mage with all the cards in clean sight and loads of time will know everything. But right now, in front of you, in game - I don't show the players the sheets of the other characters. They know what their characters see - they don't read the script.<br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":668,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/668-hoodedclaw","user":"hoodedclaw","date":". 09-24-2016, 03:20 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/668-hoodedclaw","name":"hoodedclaw","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"84"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-24T12:37:25-07:00","id":975650,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Strill</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/974857-where-is-the-horror-of-monsters-in-mage#post974857\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It goes without saying that mages are going to encounter all kinds of monsters, but that kind of horror doesn't really work in Mage. Horror movie monsters rely on the unknown. Good horror movies keep the monster hidden in the shadows, and never reveal what it actually looks like until the end. Our imaginations are much scarier than anything a costume artist could come up with. But Mages have this covered.<br /> <br /> Mages can see everything, pinpoint the nature and weaknesses of any given monster, and have all sorts of ways to become nigh-invulnerable, like turning into shadow, or shifting into twilight.</div> </div> </div> </div>Mages <i>can't </i>see everything. Not all at once, anyway. They're still human. You can only have so much focus, only so much magic active without requiring Reach. A mage with half a dozen spells active is going to be risking serious consequences every time she casts a spell. Also, from this and your other responses, it seems you've fallen into the White Room Theory Master trap: assuming any given mage has any given spell and access to all kinds of knowledge. That doesn't happen. No one mage knows everything. Heck, even with Occult 5 and a grip of specialties, a character still doesn't know everything. Mages need to study the supernatural, but that can be difficult when that inhuman force is currently trying to eat you. In-character knowledge is different from the numbers on the sheet, and even the most well-learned mages don't know it all. They might <i>pretend </i>they do, but that's probably why some mages don't live to become archmasters.<br /> <br /> Shadows and Twilight-forms are not invulnerable. They might be to a particular threat, yes, but it's okay to just let the mage win in that case. It becomes less about the horror of the monster in that scene and more about the clash of magical powers. And even then there's plenty of room to surprise the mage. Shift into shadow form? Great, but the problem is that shadowy owl-thing you're dealing with is much more at home in that state. The spirit's Influence still reaches you. Maybe you use Know Spirit to learn its Ban and Bane, and now you know its weaknesses. That doesn't save you from the streams of chemical fire it's belching your way <i>right now</i>. With time and preparation, yes, you can handily deal with this pack of ghouls, but you don't know where it's coming from, and it's hard to study them properly when they're trying to kill you and eat you, and your neighbors.<br /> <br /> Also don't forget that &quot;horror&quot; can come from revelation as much as the unknown. Let's take the first <i>Alien </i>as an example. A mage would have a much easier time seeing into the dark and in general fending off the creature, right? Now add in that director's cut scene where Ripley encounters Dallas and Brett cocooned by the Alien and being slowly transformed into eggs. <i>That's </i>where your horror is. The mage will find those unfortunate souls, but the revelation of what this thing is capable of, even if it's not quite capable of doing it to her, is horrific. Like I said in one of my responses, attack the things a mage holds dear if she herself can't be challenged. To what lengths will the character go? What if that makes her the monster?<br /> <br /> Things can still be scary even if you know a lot about them. You might be an expert in lions, but having one attacking you is still pretty terrifying. But don't fall into the trap of assuming the mage is an instant expert and has every answer needed on hand. You might have great power over shadows, and so you have a powerful weapon against that creature made of smoke and shadows, but its ability to manipulate the same can directly challenge yours. Some Mage Sight will help you understand the threat a little better, but it's not a guaranteed win. And when you do finally persevere, but you didn't kill your foe because it possessed a corpse and fled, or you destroyed it only temporarily, or realize there are others....<br /> <br /> Horror can be a little more difficult to pull off, yes, since mages have power and insight. However, that also means they can see the teeth in the shadows, and all the danger that entails. They know that the parasites eating a man's insides came from some half-flesh, half-spirit abomination, and that their magic can't cure it. At best, the mage can keep him alive with an indefinite spell of regeneration, but it may be too late. Or even her magic can't help the man's husband, who has been cocooned and transformed into another of those wasp-like things. And now the mage must kill or trap (for study, of course, totally humane experimentation...) something with the eyes and voice of a tortured human soul but the hideous body of a wasp.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Freelancer </b>\u2022 Available for work!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/109-claire-redfield","name":"Claire Redfield","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar109_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"672"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-26T11:30:56-07:00","id":976630,"title":"","content":"Fun addendum to what I said before: You can also prey on the fact you will have multiple Mages with different Sights, Gnosis, and knowledge. I'm introducing a Sin-Eater character shortly (an ambiguous Wild Card into the Seer vs Pentacle conflict currently going on). In my game Geists are Ghosts eaten by Death Spirits or vice versa. This particular Sin-Eater's Geist being of a former Mage killed by an Abyssal Entity, but that's neither here nor there. When my three protagonists see the Geist through their respective lenses, I will separately show the players three different images of a vaguely similar entity. Now, rather than our Mages actually being sure what the thing is with wondrous magic and killing suspense, they're much <i>less </i>certain of what it is they are dealing with. They only get impressions.<br /> <br /> That's the other thing about knowledge. When you acquire it, you get a sense of how much you do not have.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12121-ainsoph","name":"AinSoph","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"35"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-26T11:51:36-07:00","id":976641,"title":"","content":"I have an Obrimos running into a fight with a chupacabra that he is convinced is actually a vampire. He hasn't bothered to prepare at all other than bringing a weapon. He's just worried about the Sleeper he heard screaming in the darkness.<br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/191-strill\" style=\"background-image:url('data:image/svg+xml;base64,');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"191\" data-vbnamecard=\"191\">Strill</a> This is the difference between actual gameplay and your &quot;Mages know everything so horror sucks!&quot; scenario.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2632-mrmdubois","name":"Mrmdubois","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6827"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-26T13:35:39-07:00","id":976707,"title":"","content":"Making monsters horrifying is without a doubt harder when dealing with Mages than Vanilla mortals, after all being trapped in a dead end alley with six flesh eating zombies foot dragging their way towards you and nothing but your fists and teeth to protect you is terrifying, being trapped in that same alley with a working mini gun and the skills to use it...not so much.<br /> \u200bYou can try to tailor the monsters to your characters or design the conditions and environment to inhibit their strengths, techniques which may come across as too contrived or frustrating to enjoy, but my recommendation is to focus more on the investigative approach with your story. Focus not on fighting the monster but make most of the work about identifying and finding it. I also recommend more than one monster to give players opportunities to fight and win without ending the story, while using lesser threats to emphasize the final, greater monster waiting at the end.<br /> \u200bI also recommend skipping the axe wielding stereotype only interested in chopping up any teenagers who step into its path, instead use monsters who have plans and agendas of their own that are already works in progress when the Mages cross paths. The initial horror comes from what the monster has already done, quickly followed by the realization of what it is working to achieve, only at the end does it jump out of the shadows (or the Mages tread lightly into those shadows) and fall back on its ugly face to terrify.<br /> \u200bTo me Mages are about investigations, combat is just the occasional distraction and the last act and if violence alone can't save the day all the better.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6084-2pttakrill","name":"2ptTakrill","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"942"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-26T14:01:41-07:00","id":976728,"title":"","content":"Honestly, it's harder to make monsters horrifying for <i>anything</i> other than Vanilla mortals, Hunters included. Sure, you might not have super powers as a Hunter but as you've said, facing zombies with a working mini-gun and the skills to use it make a bit of a difference. Though as with Hunters, while it is technically harder to do, it's still very much possible to do.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2309-ashenrogue","name":"Ashenrogue","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2309_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1388"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-26T14:09:00-07:00","id":976732,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ashenrogue</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/974857-where-is-the-horror-of-monsters-in-mage?p=975063#post975063\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Yeah, but that's because people generally know exactly what zombies are capable of. They're well known, and information galore exists on them, so of course they lose a bit of that scariness of the unknown type thing, at this point they represent a completely different type of horror, which is fine. But what happens when the zombies aren't doing what you expect, and exhibit powers not native to them? How much of what you know of zombies can be relied on at the point? What happens when you shoot them in the head, destroy the brain, and they keep on coming? What if you then destroy the heart, the spinal cord, hell, even their <i>liver</i> and they still just keep coming at you? And then these mindless undead (and yes, you checked to see if they were mindless using Mind and they are) start using psychic powers against you. Who even knows what other powers they're going to bust out?</div> </div> </div> </div>What you wrote reminded me of this:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Antagonists, Page 22</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\"><i>Charlie was not a film connoisseur. He liked action movies, he like explosions. Although he rarely ventured out to to the cinema, he suddenly remembered a black and white movie he saw three decades ago.</i><br /> They're Zombies!<br /> <i>As soon as the thought entered his mind, he squeezed the trigger and the rifle fired another shot, this time penetrating the top of Alice's skull and blowing a tennis-ball-sized hole out of the back of her neck. Charlie whooped in satisfaction, but the cry died in his throat. Alice was still eating.</i></div> </div> </div> </div>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><i>\u201cYouth ages, immaturity is outgrown, ignorance can be educated, and drunkenness sobered, but stupid lasts forever.\u201d </i>~ <b>Aristophanes</b><br /> <i>&quot;Virescit Vulnere Virtus&quot;</i> ~ <b>Stewart Clan Motto</b></div> <div align=\"center\"><span style=\"font-size:18px\"><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/801713-homebrew-of-pale_crusader\" target=\"_blank\">Frailty Chronicles</a></span><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/801713-homebrew-of-pale_crusader\" target=\"_blank\">;</a><b><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/801713-homebrew-of-pale_crusader\" target=\"_blank\">Understanding the power of limitations.</a></b></div>","edited":{"userid":3279,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3279-pale_crusader","user":"Pale_Crusader","date":". 09-26-2016, 05:11 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3279-pale_crusader","name":"Pale_Crusader","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3279_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"841"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-26T14:21:02-07:00","id":976733,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ashenrogue</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/974857-where-is-the-horror-of-monsters-in-mage?p=976728#post976728\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Honestly, it's harder to make monsters horrifying for <i>anything</i> other than Vanilla mortals, Hunters included. Sure, you might not have super powers as a Hunter but as you've said, facing zombies with a working mini-gun and the skills to use it make a bit of a difference. Though as with Hunters, while it is technically harder to do, it's still very much possible to do.</div> </div> </div> </div>Ah, but &quot;horrifying&quot; doesn't necessarily <i>always </i>mean &quot;makes me afraid for my life.&quot; The mages investigating, say, an Azlu nest, and finding these cocooned bodies that are being transformed into spiders, or finding out that people have had their brains eaten by spiders who now sit in blood-soaked skull chambers controlling the body, have reason to be horrified. They may be able to defeat the Azlu, yes, but that doesn't mean it's not horrific to know that they're out there, or what they're doing, or to whom they've done it. The twisted, demonic child ghost doesn't have to pose a threat to the mage to be horrifying. The very reason it exists at all can be horrific enough.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Freelancer </b>\u2022 Available for work!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/109-claire-redfield","name":"Claire Redfield","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar109_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"672"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-26T15:31:17-07:00","id":976762,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Omegaphallic</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/974857-where-is-the-horror-of-monsters-in-mage?p=975005#post975005\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">You could create a horror so unnatural and vile, that the better you can precieve, the more damage you take. So using Mage sight or Keening on this horror actual deal damage, as the very knowledge of this thing rots the flesh of those who possess it, the deeper the knowledge the deeper the rot.<br /> <br /> A point of bashing damage for ever perception roll success, two per success is it magical perception rolls like Mage Sight, Keening, or the enhanced senses of a Werewolf or Vampire.</div> </div> </div> </div>Reminds me of the <a href=\"http://dresdenfiles.wikia.com/wiki/Naagloshii\" target=\"_blank\">Naagloshii </a>from Dresden Files and <a href=\"http://dresdenfiles.wikia.com/wiki/Sight#Turn_Coat\" target=\"_blank\">how it hurt Harry to even think about the creature he saw</a>. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_PFcOeM_Usk\" target=\"_blank\">This fits </a><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/hunter-the-vigil/963811-call-of-cthulhu-mythos-elements-in-a-hunter-game#post963811\">Lovecraftian </a><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/963812-vampire-and-the-h-p-lovecraft-mythos#post963812\">trope of</a> <a href=\"http://tvtropes.org/pmwiki/pmwiki.php/Main/GoMadFromTheRevelation\" target=\"_blank\">mere perception is dangerous</a> perfectly.<br /> <br /> One idea you could take is take the strange mutations (Represented as 1 Potency Horrors) from <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/4252-the-mythos-and-the-world-of-darkness#post4252\">The Colour from Outer Space</a> and instead have them be caused by a mutagen region of the <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/919965-2e-nemesis-continuum-updated-mechanics#post919965\">Nemesis Continuum Erosion</a>, thus investigating the cause is very dangerous.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><i>\u201cYouth ages, immaturity is outgrown, ignorance can be educated, and drunkenness sobered, but stupid lasts forever.\u201d </i>~ <b>Aristophanes</b><br /> <i>&quot;Virescit Vulnere Virtus&quot;</i> ~ <b>Stewart Clan Motto</b></div> <div align=\"center\"><span style=\"font-size:18px\"><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/801713-homebrew-of-pale_crusader\" target=\"_blank\">Frailty Chronicles</a></span><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/801713-homebrew-of-pale_crusader\" target=\"_blank\">;</a><b><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/801713-homebrew-of-pale_crusader\" target=\"_blank\">Understanding the power of limitations.</a></b></div>","edited":{"userid":3279,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3279-pale_crusader","user":"Pale_Crusader","date":". 09-26-2016, 08:00 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3279-pale_crusader","name":"Pale_Crusader","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3279_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"841"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-26T20:42:36-07:00","id":976920,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Claire Redfield</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/974857-where-is-the-horror-of-monsters-in-mage?p=976733#post976733\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Ah, but &quot;horrifying&quot; doesn't necessarily <i>always </i>mean &quot;makes me afraid for my life.&quot; The mages investigating, say, an Azlu nest, and finding these cocooned bodies that are being transformed into spiders, or finding out that people have had their brains eaten by spiders who now sit in blood-soaked skull chambers controlling the body, have reason to be horrified. They may be able to defeat the Azlu, yes, but that doesn't mean it's not horrific to know that they're out there, or what they're doing, or to whom they've done it. The twisted, demonic child ghost doesn't have to pose a threat to the mage to be horrifying. The very reason it exists at all can be horrific enough.</div> </div> </div> </div>Oh I totally agree, but people seem to be employing a fairly specific use of the term so I'm trying to use that meaning instead of listing how it can be horrifying in soooo many other ways.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2309-ashenrogue","name":"Ashenrogue","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2309_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1388"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-26T23:06:37-07:00","id":976995,"title":"","content":"How to make a monster interesting, I think, can all be summed up in one sentence included in the original Ravenloft. A sentence that changed RPG games.<br /> &quot;Strahd is a genius, so play him like one.&quot;<br /> \u200bThe key to making a monster interesting isn't a cool ability or twist to its appearance, the key to making him interesting is having him do something interesting. Jason chopping up random teens quickly gets boring, requiring increasing gore and tits to keep teenagers watching. A monster with a foreword thinking plan and the ability to react intelligently to obstacles is far more interesting.<br /> The ability to speak also helps.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6084-2pttakrill","name":"2ptTakrill","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"942"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-27T03:22:55-07:00","id":977091,"title":"","content":"It seems to me there is a degree of arguing past each other going on in this thread. The OP is basically saying that it's possible to &quot;kite&quot; a lot of monsters if you're a mage. That you can do something like turn to shadow and kill them off at your leisure. Like running around the Big Bad in a computer game peppering them with arrows from out of range. The OP is asking if that's really the case and if so how you can deal with it. They're also wary of things that feel like the GM just nerfing the player's ideas / abilties and coming across as saying &quot;nuh-uh, I've decided that this monster can hit you whilst you're smoke just because I don't want you doing that anymore&quot;. And all of that is valid. I don't think telling the OP they're wrong in this helps because it's kind of true.<br /> <br /> But on the other hand what you have is a lot of people saying that's not really the point. That you shouldn't BE trying to challenge a mage with things like this. That there are lots of creatures and situations where this wont help.<br /> <br /> I think the connection that might help bridge the two view points is to say this should be established from the outset in a game. If you start with gnashy-clawed monsters and that's your game for a while and then suddenly PCs figure out they can Smoke N' Kite once they get the power, and THEN the GM suddenly starts throwing in creatures that can bypass the PCs, then yes players probably will feel that you're out to get them. Or if all of a sudden monsters are hidden and ambushing all the time - same thing. What you need to do is establish before you get to this point that the game is one of mysteries and finding the monster or figuring out the key to defeating it. If you want to scare the players by having loved ones in danger, you need to get those in early and build up their relationship, not just throw in a girlfriend one adventure and then threaten her.<br /> <br /> A lot of this thread reads like the following loop:<br /> OP: This is busted.<br /> Forum: We don't care.<br /> OP: But it's busted.<br /> Forum: But we don't care.<br /> Repeat.<br /> <br /> OP is right - it <b>is</b> hard to straight-up Scary Mysterious Monster the PCs in a mage game. Respondents are also right - you have to work around that. The missing part is helping bridge the expectation gap. How does a GM stop the players feeling like the GM is nerfing them. My answer is that you have to establish in advance and early what the nature of a Mage game is, not just knee-jerk once they discover how to turn into Shadows. And if it has got to that point, then you have to address it explicitly with your players and say &quot;I realized with the powers you have that the game isn't going to keep working the way it has done so I want to try a more puzzle / social / whatever approach to adventures.&quot;.<br /> <br /> Anyway, just some impressions. They could be off-base.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#D3D3D3\"><i>Fnord!</i></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11763-knasser","name":"knasser","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11763_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"39"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-27T03:47:18-07:00","id":977095,"title":"","content":"There have actually been at least two different kinds of responses, one is that Mage isn't the kind of game where the horror necessarily comes from the monsters. Two if you want the horror to come from the monsters then in light of the ability of Mages you don't target Mages directly, or if you do then mess with their assumptions about what they're up against. There have been multiple variations on those two basic premises. In either case it's not talking past Strill, because the real problem with his stance was that he was making the assumption that because Mages can demonstrate enormous power and foresight that it must be the case that they always do which isn't at all realistic.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2632-mrmdubois","name":"Mrmdubois","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6827"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-27T07:36:46-07:00","id":977173,"title":"","content":"Pretty much what Mrmdubois said. Mages have the ability to know a lot and to figure out a lot, <i>but</i>, they do not know everything about everything. Sure, if they had the luxury of time and circumstance to be able to comfortably study whatever monster that tickles their fancy that week then sure, they could probably learn a lot about it and take away that unknown factor, but so could a vanilla mortal with a dot or 2 in Occult. If you have the luxury to do so then just about anyone could discover a great deal with enough time. <br /> <br /> So the answer that has been given is to take that luxury away. Throw and unknown at them and give them a reason to confront it then and there. Don't give them time to sit back casting every Knowing spell in existence and then some. And again, whatever they're watching doesn't have to be attacking them specifically, make it so that there are other people who could be saved if they acted as quickly as possible, and give them the choice on staying back to cast more spells and learn more or to go out and save lives. If they choose to charge in anyways, they face the horror of the unknown, if they choose to sit back, observe, and let whoever it is being attacked die...well then we've got ourselves a completely different type of horror story now don't we?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2309-ashenrogue","name":"Ashenrogue","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2309_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1388"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-27T09:21:08-07:00","id":977212,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>knasser</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/974857-where-is-the-horror-of-monsters-in-mage?p=977091#post977091\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It seems to me there is a degree of arguing past each other going on in this thread. The OP is basically saying that it's possible to &quot;kite&quot; a lot of monsters if you're a mage. That you can do something like turn to shadow and kill them off at your leisure. Like running around the Big Bad in a computer game peppering them with arrows from out of range. The OP is asking if that's really the case and if so how you can deal with it. They're also wary of things that feel like the GM just nerfing the player's ideas / abilties and coming across as saying &quot;nuh-uh, I've decided that this monster can hit you whilst you're smoke just because I don't want you doing that anymore&quot;. And all of that is valid. I don't think telling the OP they're wrong in this helps because it's kind of true.<br /> <br /> But on the other hand what you have is a lot of people saying that's not really the point. That you shouldn't BE trying to challenge a mage with things like this. That there are lots of creatures and situations where this wont help.<br /> <br /> I think the connection that might help bridge the two view points is to say this should be established from the outset in a game. If you start with gnashy-clawed monsters and that's your game for a while and then suddenly PCs figure out they can Smoke N' Kite once they get the power, and THEN the GM suddenly starts throwing in creatures that can bypass the PCs, then yes players probably will feel that you're out to get them. Or if all of a sudden monsters are hidden and ambushing all the time - same thing. What you need to do is establish before you get to this point that the game is one of mysteries and finding the monster or figuring out the key to defeating it. If you want to scare the players by having loved ones in danger, you need to get those in early and build up their relationship, not just throw in a girlfriend one adventure and then threaten her.<br /> <br /> A lot of this thread reads like the following loop:<br /> OP: This is busted.<br /> Forum: We don't care.<br /> OP: But it's busted.<br /> Forum: But we don't care.<br /> Repeat.<br /> <br /> OP is right - it <b>is</b> hard to straight-up Scary Mysterious Monster the PCs in a mage game. Respondents are also right - you have to work around that. The missing part is helping bridge the expectation gap. How does a GM stop the players feeling like the GM is nerfing them. My answer is that you have to establish in advance and early what the nature of a Mage game is, not just knee-jerk once they discover how to turn into Shadows. And if it has got to that point, then you have to address it explicitly with your players and say &quot;I realized with the powers you have that the game isn't going to keep working the way it has done so I want to try a more puzzle / social / whatever approach to adventures.&quot;.<br /> <br /> Anyway, just some impressions. They could be off-base.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Personally, I don't think the point of M:tAw is that Mages run into beings that scare them very often. The scary thing that Mages have to deal with is Wisdom degeneration because there are simply things that humans were never meant to know and Mages have a knack for looking for and at those things. In my games I often liken Paradox, the Abyss, and things like it to Lovecraft's Ancient Ones. I'm also not afraid to toss gribblies into my stories that cause the Frightened Condition or Insane Tilt on sight in humans. I think that maybe this is a problem of gribbly design rather than the game straight up lacking something.<br /> <br /> Other than that, pretty much agree with what you've laid out here. A good storyteller needs to establish and manage their player's expectations.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1630-jacob","name":"Jacob","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1630_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"681"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-27T20:55:51-07:00","id":977580,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>2ptTakrill</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/974857-where-is-the-horror-of-monsters-in-mage?p=976995#post976995\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">A monster with a foreword thinking plan and the ability to react intelligently to obstacles is far more interesting.<br /> The ability to speak also helps.</div> </div> </div> </div><b>*Points to the infinite number of God Machine plots, a fraction of which has the ultimate fate of the human race at stakes*</b> - The Aztecs had something going with killing all the pretty teens.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12191-malus","name":"Malus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12191_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1562"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-28T00:53:36-07:00","id":977652,"title":"","content":"An aside but you can add a fun scary twist to a monster by having some off-screen influence control it. You're fighting a dumb, ravenous beast so how come it always knows when and where to attack or how did it escape the locked room you trapped it in? If anyone has ever seen the old film Les Pactes des Loups (for the sake of all that is holy on this Earth, do NOT watch the dubbed version) you know what I mean. It adds both mystery and fear.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#D3D3D3\"><i>Fnord!</i></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11763-knasser","name":"knasser","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11763_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"39"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-28T16:03:20-07:00","id":978017,"title":"","content":"Is Mages knowing everything any more of an issue then a high clarity Darkling Oracle? Think about how powerful that Changeling can Keen.<br /> <br /> I think for anyone still worried about Mage's not being scared, should look at the Tokyo setting, plenty of places Mages magic don't reach, Toyko has a ton of opportunities for Mages, its also where they are most vulnerible.<br /> <br /> Tokyo should solve most of their problems, plus give them some interesting stuff to check.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2241-omegaphallic","name":"Omegaphallic","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3435"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-28T22:24:54-07:00","id":978214,"title":"","content":"You know, as much shit as I've started complaining about Mage, I've never felt like Mage lacked adequately monstrous antagonists. <br /> <br /> The Abyss is absolutely fantastic, and the whole reality-flensing nature of it makes it something Mages really do have a hard time handling, and its presence is often their fault. The Seers, Scelesti, and Banishers have the same powers that the players do, so that's an even match right there. <br /> <br /> I mean, I'd personally nerf mages if you're going to be putting them with other splats or monsters from other lines, but even if you don't they could still be seriously boned by quite a few things. Summoning silver inside a werewolf's body is pretty cheap, but when there are four other werewolves, only two of which you noticed...","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5358-geckopirateship","name":"Geckopirateship","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5358_2.gif","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1383"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-29T16:49:13-07:00","id":978713,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>knasser</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/974857-where-is-the-horror-of-monsters-in-mage?p=977091#post977091\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">OP is right - it <b>is</b> hard to straight-up Scary Mysterious Monster the PCs in a mage game. Respondents are also right - you have to work around that. The missing part is helping bridge the expectation gap. How does a GM stop the players feeling like the GM is nerfing them. My answer is that you have to establish in advance and early what the nature of a Mage game is, not just knee-jerk once they discover how to turn into Shadows. And if it has got to that point, then you have to address it explicitly with your players and say &quot;I realized with the powers you have that the game isn't going to keep working the way it has done so I want to try a more puzzle / social / whatever approach to adventures.&quot;.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think a two disclaimers would fix this problem of expectation:<br /> <br /> Evolving ecosystems and economies (supernatural or otherwise) do not permit unfilled niches or power vacuums for long.<br /> <br /> In a setting with many mystery solvers, any unsolved mysteries must be unsolved for a reason.<br /> <br /> These act as reminders in setting design for the storyteller while providing a backbone to his narrative style, and cautionary warnings to player that their characters exist in a world that is not an empty abyss but rather a place were things go on outside the limelight which follows them through the world.<br /> <br /> If Kiting is possible within the setting then it should be addressed in the setting as if it was a thing possible. This isn't <a href=\"http://www.imdb.com/title/tt1058017/\" target=\"_blank\">The Invention of Lying</a> where the players are the protagonists and thus the only one to come up with an obvious, semi-obvious, plausible, or even outlandish idea. In real life, a world of people spend all day trying to think of ideas to get ahead, assuming the PCs are the only proactive dynamic thinkers in the game universe is hugely condescending to the rest of the story setting.<br /> <br /> EDIT: Actually thinking about it the above assume a High Prep Storytelling play style because of forethought required to simulate the change of consequences found in complex ecosystems and economies. A more improvisational approach is as valid but relies on a completely different set of strengths and has different weaknesses , but is compatible with the above suggestion with storytelling legerdemain and chicanery. As such I'll add a disclaimer to my purposed solution: Your Mileage May Vary.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><i>\u201cYouth ages, immaturity is outgrown, ignorance can be educated, and drunkenness sobered, but stupid lasts forever.\u201d </i>~ <b>Aristophanes</b><br /> <i>&quot;Virescit Vulnere Virtus&quot;</i> ~ <b>Stewart Clan Motto</b></div> <div align=\"center\"><span style=\"font-size:18px\"><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/801713-homebrew-of-pale_crusader\" target=\"_blank\">Frailty Chronicles</a></span><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/801713-homebrew-of-pale_crusader\" target=\"_blank\">;</a><b><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/801713-homebrew-of-pale_crusader\" target=\"_blank\">Understanding the power of limitations.</a></b></div>","edited":{"userid":3279,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3279-pale_crusader","user":"Pale_Crusader","date":". 06-29-2017, 09:28 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3279-pale_crusader","name":"Pale_Crusader","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3279_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"841"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-29T11:31:26-07:00","id":1109335,"title":"","content":"Cogitohazards, things that harm you by knowing about them. These types of things <i>ruin</i> mages, because they are so good at knowing things and mages ignore the common sense that somethings were not meant to be known, because of their hubris. You need the Lovecraftian theme which means certain monsters are the source of mental degradation if it is known, for the best monster horror in Mage. Cosmic horror linked to a creature not-meant-to-be-known.<br /> <br /> Case in point...<br /> <br /> <b><span style=\"font-family:inherit\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\">Slenderman</span></span></b><br /> <b>Mental Attributes: Intelligence </b>2, <b>Wits </b>3, <b>Resolve </b>3<br /> <b>Physical Attributes: Strength</b> 6, <b>Dexterity </b>3, <b>Stamina</b> 5<br /> <b>Social Attributes: Presence</b> 4, <b>Manipulation </b>1, <b>Composure </b>10<br /> <b>Mental Skills: Investigation</b> 5 (Trailing), <b>Medicine </b>3 (Removing Organs)<br /> <b>Physical Skills: Athletics </b>5 (Chasing others), <b>Brawl </b>3 (Breaking limbs), <b>Stealth </b>5 (Blending in the background)<br /> <b>Social Skills: Intimidation </b>5 (Lurking dread), <b>Empathy </b>3 (Comforting children)<br /> <b>Merits: Fame</b> 3, <b>Mystery Cult Initiation </b>5 (<a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/799082-cofd-diy-mystery-cults?p=1108502#post1108502\">Slender Man Cult</a>), <b>Trained Observer </b>3,<b> Relentless</b> 1, <b>Greyhound </b>1<br /> <b>Potency: </b>6<br /> <b>Willpower: </b>13<br /> <b>Virtue: </b>Patient<br /> <b>Vice: </b>Fitful<br /> <b>Aspirations:</b> Watching cult members, Abducting innocents, Entering Scenes of those with Slender Sickness<br /> <b>Initiative: </b>13<br /> <b>Defense: </b> 8 (10 When Reality Stutter)<br /> <b>Size: </b>7<br /> <b>Speed: </b>19 (Species Factor 10)<br /> <b>Health:</b> 12<br /> <b>Weapons/Attacks:<br /> Type Damage Range Dice Pool</b><br /> <b>Featureless white hands</b> +0B Melee 10<br /> <b>Evicerating Shadow Tendrils</b> +1L Melee 10<br /> <b>Bans: </b>Never injure a child<br /> <b>Banes:</b> Have no one know about it<br /> <b>Dread Powers:<br /> REALITY STUTTER</b><br /> The creature\u2019s presence sets reality stuttering and convulsing, allowing it to flicker from place to place. By reflexively spending 1 Willpower when it moves, the creature can translocate itself to any location it can see up to its Speed in meters away, seeming to observers to just spasm in and out of reality as it goes. Doing so adds +2 to its Defense for the turn.<br /> <b>SURPRISE ENTRANCE</b><br /> No matter how secure its victims think they are, by spending 1 Willpower the creature may suddenly appear in the scene. It can manifest out of sight (either of a being or device) in any scene where someone has the Slender Sickness Condition, and then REALITY STUTTER past any barrier. Any character witnessing this entrance must succeed on a reflexive Resolve + Composure roll or gain the Shaken Condition.<br /> If the characters have actively taken appropriate measures to keep the Horror out (barring and locking the door, running recording equipment, etc.) it will take the creature one full turn to break through, giving the characters time to react. If not, it\u2019s just there as a reflexive action and can take one more action before anyone can react.<br /> <b>JUMP SCARE</b><br /> The creature appears suddenly or lurches into abrupt action just when you thought it was down for the count. By spending 1 Willpower, the creature may resolve the Shaken condition on another character, choosing which action will automatically fail. The character still earns a Beat as normal. Alternately, the creature may spend 1 Willpower to turn the automatic failure caused by a character resolving the Shaken Condition into a dramatic failure. In this case, the character earns two Beats (one for resolving the Condition, one for the dramatic failure).<br /> <b>IMMORTAL</b><br /> The creature isn\u2019t bound by mortal laws of life and death. It\u2019s not indestructible, per se, but if it\u2019s killed or incapacitated by anything other than its Bane, it just comes back in a later scene like nothing happened. Even if it\u2019s destroyed with its Bane, in this case eradicating all knowledge of it, it\u2019s never really gone \u2014 all it takes is someone learning of its existence to bring the creature back for a sequel story.<br /> <br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:inherit\"><i><span style=\"font-family:inherit\"><span style=\"font-family:inherit\">Originally posted by <b>Pale_Crusader</b> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/799082-cofd-diy-mystery-cults?p=1108502#post1108502\">View Post</a></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:inherit\"><span style=\"font-family:inherit\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\"><b>---Side Bar---</b></span></span><br /> <b>Where's the Telepathic Powers?</b><br /> Slenderman is known for his powerful telepathic mind control. People blame Slenderman for possessing them, for mind controlling them, for forcing them to do the awful things they do in his name. The tragic truth is he has no such power other than his frightening presence and he never even makes requests. His presence is supernaturally terrifying, and having Slender Sickness disrupts normal thinking but does not rob its victims of agency. The horrible things Slenderman followers do they decide for themselves. He stalks, he lurks, he harries and terrifies but ultimately his victims do the most monstrous things all on their own.<br /> <span style=\"font-family:inherit\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\"><b>----------------</b></span></span></span></span></i></span><br /> <br /> <b>SLENDER SICKNESS CONDITION</b><br /> Those with this Condition are valid targets of Slenderman's SURPRISE ENTRANCE Dread Power. Also at the beginning of each session the player of the character chooses one of the follow conditions to apply at some point during that session: Frightened, Lost, Shaken, or Spooked. The Storyteller chooses when that happens in the story with a fleeting glimpse of Slenderman. If the character doesn't have a player associated with it mechanically (meaning she isn't a PC, nor does she represent a Merit, Touchstone or similar mechanic), the Storyteller chooses the Condition.<br /> <b>Cause:</b> Those that actively investigate the truth that Slenderman exists and believe it gain this condition. It is not enough to be told, the person with this condition has researched and earnestly sought out proof of the rumors and urban legends and found it. Mechanically this can be handled with the Clue system in CodD page 81, requiring 3 Clues (or for Mages the use of <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/886930-sample-mysteries?p=1108928#post1108928\">Focused Mage Sight with a Mystery Opacity of 1</a>).<br /> <b>Resolution:</b> Gain the Amnesia Condition concerning everything they know about Slenderman (if they lose that Condition they regain this one)<br /> <b>Beat: </b>Have Slenderman use SURPRISE ENTRANCE Dread Power to enter a Scene with the character. (The other Conditions gained by this Condition grant Beats normally).<br /> <br /> It can be argued <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=JS7GZhNb7eM\" target=\"_blank\">EverymanHYBRID</a> is about a cabal of cocky Free Council mages with Fate, Space, Mind, and Time dealing with this problem.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>H.P. Lovecraft</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">The most merciful thing in the world, I think, is the inability of the human mind to correlate all its contents.</div> </div> </div> </div>Mages have less of that mercy, not more, thus making them prime targets of Lovecraftian horror.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><i>\u201cYouth ages, immaturity is outgrown, ignorance can be educated, and drunkenness sobered, but stupid lasts forever.\u201d </i>~ <b>Aristophanes</b><br /> <i>&quot;Virescit Vulnere Virtus&quot;</i> ~ <b>Stewart Clan Motto</b></div> <div align=\"center\"><span style=\"font-size:18px\"><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/801713-homebrew-of-pale_crusader\" target=\"_blank\">Frailty Chronicles</a></span><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/801713-homebrew-of-pale_crusader\" target=\"_blank\">;</a><b><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/801713-homebrew-of-pale_crusader\" target=\"_blank\">Understanding the power of limitations.</a></b></div>","edited":{"userid":3279,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3279-pale_crusader","user":"Pale_Crusader","date":". 06-29-2017, 05:07 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3279-pale_crusader","name":"Pale_Crusader","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3279_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"841"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-29T12:15:15-07:00","id":1109342,"title":"","content":"....Why the need to respond to a long defunct thread?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kelly R.S. Steele, Freelance Writer(Feel free to call me Kelly, Arcane, or Arc)</b><br /> The world is not beautiful, therefore it is.-Keiichi Sigsawa, Kino's Journey<br /> Feminine pronouns, please.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","name":"ArcaneArts","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar99_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11384"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-29T18:25:09-07:00","id":1109525,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>ArcaneArts</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/974857-where-is-the-horror-of-monsters-in-mage?p=1109342#post1109342\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">....Why the need to respond to a long defunct thread?</div> </div> </div> </div>To be frank I did not check dates, this thread popped in a search for &quot;Horror&quot; in the titles of NWoD of threads because I wanted my Slender man interpretation to show up in a thread about compiled Horrors. Looked and looked and strangely didn't find one but I found this thread and was really excited so I posted before reading to the end. Having posted I noticed I was the last person to post before me and was embarrassed. I stand by my posts, and it is interesting to see how my reply a year later differs based on my how I think now vs then.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><i>\u201cYouth ages, immaturity is outgrown, ignorance can be educated, and drunkenness sobered, but stupid lasts forever.\u201d </i>~ <b>Aristophanes</b><br /> <i>&quot;Virescit Vulnere Virtus&quot;</i> ~ <b>Stewart Clan Motto</b></div> <div align=\"center\"><span style=\"font-size:18px\"><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/801713-homebrew-of-pale_crusader\" target=\"_blank\">Frailty Chronicles</a></span><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/801713-homebrew-of-pale_crusader\" target=\"_blank\">;</a><b><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/801713-homebrew-of-pale_crusader\" target=\"_blank\">Understanding the power of limitations.</a></b></div>","edited":{"userid":3279,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3279-pale_crusader","user":"Pale_Crusader","date":". 06-29-2017, 09:29 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3279-pale_crusader","name":"Pale_Crusader","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3279_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"841"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-29T18:34:26-07:00","id":1109527,"title":"","content":"It's pretty good. Thinking about it, I think your version of the Slender Man might make a really good Bound with very little modification. All it really